# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  What git is told to leave alone, and why.
#
#  Every entry here is something THIS project actually produces, or something it must never contain.
#  That distinction is the point of the file: a rule for something that cannot happen is a rule that
#  stops being read, and the twenty lines nobody reads are the reason the one that mattered is missed.
#
#  Nothing has to be committed for the application to run from a clone. `start.bat` does every step it
#  finds missing — it creates the environment, installs both dependency trees, unpacks ffmpeg if the
#  machine has none, and builds the interface. That is what makes every artefact below disposable.
#
#  To find out which rule caught a path, and from which line of this file:
#
#      git check-ignore -v <path>
#
#  To commit something one of these rules is holding back, name it with `!` at the end of this file
#  rather than reaching for `git add --force`: an exception that is written down is one the next
#  person can find.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# --- Python: the environment, and what the interpreter writes as it runs ----------------------
#  `venv/` is created by `start.bat` and installed from `backend/requirements.txt`, which *is*
#  tracked. Committing the environment would put another machine's compiled binaries in the history
#  and still not be the environment anyone else needs.
venv/
.venv/
env/
ENV/
#  Bytecode and the interpreter's own output: regenerated on import, and they embed absolute paths.
#  `[cod]` covers `.pyd` as well, the Windows extension modules.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.so

# --- Tests: the cache, and nothing else --------------------------------------------------------
#  `backend/tests` and `specs/features` are source and are tracked. This is the cache pytest leaves
#  behind, which describes one machine's last run and is regenerated by the next one.
.pytest_cache/

# --- Node and the interface --------------------------------------------------------------------
#  Both dependency trees are installed by `start.bat` from the two tracked lock files, which is what
#  makes an install reproducible. The lock files stay; the trees do not.
node_modules/
frontend/node_modules/
#  `frontend/dist` is the built interface and the engine serves it to the window. `start.bat`
#  rebuilds it whenever a source file is newer than the bundle, so it is never stale in a clone for
#  longer than one launch.
frontend/dist/
.vite/
frontend/.vite/
*.tsbuildinfo
.eslintcache

# --- The tools start.bat installs when a machine has none --------------------------------------
#  A portable Node and ffmpeg, plus the archives they came from: about a quarter of a gigabyte, put
#  there by `scripts/bootstrap.ps1` for a machine that had neither. Committing it would put somebody
#  else's platform-specific binaries in the history for no gain; running `start.bat` again
#  reproduces all of it, which is the whole point of that file.
.tools/

# --- Media, and the one accident this repository is most exposed to ----------------------------
#
#  This repository holds no footage, on purpose: nothing in it needs a media file to run, and the
#  checks that do use media **build their own fixtures** with ffmpeg into the system temp folder
#  (`scripts/check_normalize.py`) rather than committing any. So a media file appearing in this
#  working tree is an accident, and the accident is large.
#
#  The product writes its output *beside its source* — that is the one place it writes, by design.
#  Point it at a file that happens to live inside this checkout and a 4 GB master plus a WAV and an
#  MP3 land in the tree, one `git add -A` from a history nobody can shrink. So the extensions are
#  refused up front.
*.mp4
*.mov
*.mkv
*.m4v
*.avi
*.webm
*.wmv
*.flv
*.mpg
*.mpeg
*.vob
*.m2ts
*.3gp
#  `.ts` and `.mts` are both MPEG transport streams and both TypeScript source extensions, and this
#  project writes TypeScript. Neither is listed: a rule for either would ignore the interface's own
#  source, silently, from the first commit after it was added. `.m2ts` above is the unambiguous
#  form, and a bare transport stream is not a name this product writes.
#
#  The sound, the sound files it writes beside a master, and the captions that travel with a video:
#  refused with it, because the whole point of these rules is that a file arriving beside another
#  file arrives as a pair.
*.wav
*.mp3
*.m4a
*.aac
*.flac
*.ogg
*.opus
*.srt
*.vtt
*.ass
*.ssa
#  A folder to put any of it in deliberately, so the rules above are for the accidental case.
/media/
/footage/

#  The name a run gives its own output, caught as well as the extensions above — because it says
#  *which* files those are without consulting the extension list, and because the run also writes a
#  temporary `.part` file and a `.normalize-*` directory beside the destination while it works.
* - normalized.*
* - normalized *.*
*.part.*
.normalize-*/

#  Where the engine's own temporary directory goes, and why it is not listed above: `run_normalize`
#  creates it with `tempfile.mkdtemp(prefix=".normalize-")` **beside the output**, so that the rename
#  which publishes the result is a move within one volume. It names it `.normalize-` and removes it
#  on every path — success, failure and cancellation alike — so the rule above is a belt for a
#  braces that has never been needed, and it is here because the one time it is needed it is a
#  directory full of somebody's audio in the working tree.

# --- Packaging output --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  `electron-builder` writes here — `build.directories.output` in package.json — for the `--dir` form
#  and for the installer alike: the unpacked app, the installer, its blockmap and the channel files
#  generated beside them. None of it is source and all of it is reproduced by `npm run dist`.
/dist/
/release/
/out/
*.blockmap
builder-effective-config.yaml
electron-builder.env

# --- Anything that could carry a secret --------------------------------------------------------
#  Nothing in this project needs a file like these today, and that is the reason to refuse them now
#  rather than after somebody adds a signing key to make one machine work: a secret committed once is
#  in the history for good, because removing it in a later commit does not remove it from the past.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
*.pfx
*.p12
*.keystore
secrets.json

# --- Logs and leftovers ------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  A log is written by whatever was being debugged and describes one run. `nul` is the Windows one
#  worth naming: `> nul` where `>nul` was meant creates a file called `nul`, which no Windows program
#  can then open or delete by name.
*.log
npm-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
pnpm-debug.log*
*.orig
*.rej
*.bak
*.tmp
*.temp
nul

# --- Editors -----------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  `.vscode/` is ignored rather than the whole thing, so a shared extension list can still be
#  committed deliberately.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/settings.json
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
*.sublime-workspace
*.sublime-project

# --- Operating system --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  `Thumbs.db` and `desktop.ini` are written into any folder Windows has been asked to draw,
#  including this one, just by looking at it.
.DS_Store
.AppleDouble
.LSOverride
._*
.Trashes
Thumbs.db
ehthumbs.db
Desktop.ini
$RECYCLE.BIN/
*.stackdump

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#  The exceptions, and why each one is a decision rather than a convenience.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

#  `docs/shots/` holds the screenshots `README.md` and `docs/TRUTH.md` refer to, captured by
#  `scripts/check_window.py` from the live page. They are evidence for a claim — "the interface has
#  been visually inspected" — so they are source, and they are small. The rule above that would
#  catch them is the media block, and this has to come after it.
!docs/shots/
!docs/shots/*.png
